;; no-c-tls — engenho asks for no C TLS stack: reqwest is rustls-only, rustls
;; runs on ring, and no OpenSSL, native-tls or AWS-LC crate enters Cargo.lock
;; through an edge nobody has read.
;;
;; TWO C stacks, not one. `tls:BANNED-NATIVE` is reqwest's native-tls backend
;; and the OpenSSL it links; `tls:BANNED-AWS-LC` is AWS-LC, the C library
;; aws-lc-sys builds with `cc` and aws-lc-rs binds. They arrive by different
;; feature paths, from the same upstream project, and both are recorded below.
;;
;; WHY (improvement plan T0.8, buildci-5; integration rows I34, tls-02).
;;   reqwest's default features include `default-tls`, which is native-tls:
;;   OpenSSL through openssl-sys on Linux, Security.framework on darwin.
;;   Wherever `default-tls` is on, reqwest's `TlsBackend::default()` picks
;;   native-tls even when a rustls feature is enabled too, so a
;;   `Client::new()` or a bare `Client::builder()` runs on it. engenho has two
;;   of those in the daemon: the apiserver's webhook-admission client and the
;;   kubelet's HTTPS probe client. Cargo unifies features, so ONE dependency
;;   that asks reqwest for its defaults turns `default-tls` on for every crate
;;   in the build, whatever the workspace line says.
;;   * The workspace line already said `default-features = false`, but named
;;     only json and rustls-tls. charset, http2 and system-proxy reached
;;     engenho only because another crate asked for all of reqwest's
;;     defaults; fixing that crate would have silently dropped HTTP/2.
;;     The line now names them, plus rustls-tls-native-roots (the OS trust
;;     store native-tls read), as pleme-io/sui 66a289f did.
;;   * That other crate is sui-store 0.1.153, reached through
;;     engenho-fonte-cli -> engenho-fonte (with-sui-eval) ->
;;     engenho-sui-typescape -> sui-eval. It asks reqwest for its defaults.
;;     sui fixed its workspace line in 66a289f, and no published sui release
;;     carries that commit (the newest, 0.1.219, still asks). Cargo.gen.lock
;;     is ONE workspace-wide resolve, and Nix builds every crate from it.
;;     It lists `default` and `default-tls` among reqwest's features, so
;;     every Nix-built binary that uses reqwest compiles native-tls. On
;;     Linux that links OpenSSL. Measured 2026-09-19: the x86_64-linux
;;     engenho-mcp derivation depends on rust_openssl-sys, which is built
;;     against openssl-static 3.6.2. engenho-mcp does not even reach
;;     sui-store. A per-package `cargo tree` shows none of this.
;;   * `cargo tree -i openssl-sys --workspace` cannot see any of this on
;;     darwin: native-tls uses Security.framework there, so the host-only
;;     query prints nothing. It needs `--target all`. Cargo.lock is resolved
;;     for every target at once, which is why this check reads it.
;;
;; WHY, AWS-LC (tls-02). aws-lc-sys 0.41.0 is AWS-LC's C, compiled by its
;;   build script; aws-lc-rs 1.17.0 is the binding, and rustls's DEFAULT
;;   crypto provider. The workspace rustls line has asked for `ring` with
;;   `default-features = false` throughout, and the daemon installs ring as
;;   the process provider at boot — and aws-lc-sys is in Cargo.lock anyway,
;;   because feature unification does not care what our line says.
;;   Measured 2026-09-19 with
;;     cargo tree --workspace --all-features --target all -i aws-lc-rs -e features
;;   and again with default features. Both give ONE root, four edges:
;;     aws-lc-rs 1.17.0  -> aws-lc-sys, through aws-lc-rs's own `aws-lc-sys`
;;                          feature, which rustls's `aws_lc_rs` turns on.
;;     rustls 0.23.40    <- feature `aws_lc_rs` (its aliases: `aws-lc-rs`;
;;                          implied by `default`, `prefer-post-quantum`,
;;                          `fips`).
;;     rustls-webpki 0.103.13 <- feature `aws-lc-rs`, which rustls's
;;                          `aws_lc_rs` enables as `webpki/aws-lc-rs`.
;;     quinn-proto 0.11.14 <- its optional `aws-lc-rs` dependency. NOT
;;                          activated in any engenho build: it appears in the
;;                          lock because Cargo.lock is resolved with every
;;                          optional dependency, and in no `cargo tree` run.
;;   The one root that turns rustls's `aws_lc_rs` on:
;;     sui-eval 0.1.153 (default)
;;       -> gix `blocking-http-transport-reqwest-rust-tls`
;;       -> gix-transport 0.57.1 `http-client-reqwest-rust-tls`
;;       -> reqwest 0.13.4 `rustls` = `__rustls-aws-lc-rs`
;;       -> rustls/aws-lc-rs + tokio-rustls/aws-lc-rs + hyper-rustls/aws-lc-rs
;;   Two traps in that chain. reqwest 0.13 renamed its backends: `default-tls`
;;   there is `["rustls"]`, and the plain `rustls` feature is the aws-lc-rs
;;   one, while `rustls-tls` (what the workspace line asks reqwest 0.12 for)
;;   is the ring one. And engenho reaches this through reqwest 0.13.4, a
;;   SECOND reqwest in the lock, not the 0.12.28 the workspace line pins —
;;   so the attribution table below, which reads dependents of the reqwest
;;   that carries native-tls, never sees it. That is why the aws-lc family is
;;   recorded as tls:CARRIERS rows over the edge, not as attribution rows.
;;   Nothing in this workspace can turn it off: cargo unifies rustls's
;;   features across the build, and gix-transport asks for the aws-lc one.
;;
;; RULES:
;;   workspace   every [workspace.dependencies] line in tls:WORKSPACE-LINES
;;               (reqwest, rustls) is a table with `default-features = false`
;;               (the boolean), names each feature that line wants, and names
;;               none of that dependency's tls:C-FEATURES.
;;   members     no member manifest asks a guarded dependency for its default
;;               or C-crypto features. Every reqwest and rustls entry, also
;;               one renamed through `package = "…"`, in [dependencies],
;;               [dev-dependencies], [build-dependencies] or a
;;               [target.*.…] form of them: either inherits the workspace
;;               line (`workspace = true`) without setting
;;               `default-features = true`, or sets `default-features =
;;               false` itself. No entry lists a tls:C-FEATURES feature of
;;               that dependency, and no [features] entry enables one through
;;               `reqwest/…`, `rustls/…` or their `?/…` forms.
;;   present     (positive control) Cargo.lock resolves each of
;;               tls:REQUIRED. Without them the ban would pass because there
;;               is no rustls HTTP client at all.
;;   direct      every Cargo.lock package that depends on a tls:BANNED crate
;;               is itself banned, or has a tls:CARRIERS row for that exact
;;               (package, version, banned crate) edge. Anything else pulls a
;;               C crypto stack in by itself and fails.
;;   attributed  while a tls:BANNED-NATIVE crate is in Cargo.lock, every
;;               non-workspace package that depends on a reqwest carrying a
;;               banned edge has a tls:ATTRIBUTION row for its exact locked
;;               version, and at least one of those rows says `asks`. A
;;               package with no row fails: Cargo.lock does not record which
;;               dependent turned `default-tls` on, so a dependent nobody has
;;               read is treated as a new source.
;;   stale       an attribution row that matches no such dependent fails.
;;               Once no tls:BANNED-NATIVE crate is in Cargo.lock, that is
;;               every row: a row left behind would excuse the next
;;               regression.
;;
;; THE CARRIER TABLE. Each row is (package version banned-crate why): one
;; read edge from a crate that is not itself C into one that is, with the
;; feature that opens it. It permits that EDGE and nothing else — not the
;; package at another version, not another banned crate — so a lock bump
;; leaves the bumped package unattributed and fails, which is the prompt to
;; read the new manifest. A row whose edge has left the lock is inert rather
;; than a hole, because it cannot excuse a different dependent; delete it
;; when the reading it records stops being true.
;;
;; THE ATTRIBUTION TABLE. Each row is (package version kind why). The kind
;; is `asks` (the package's own manifest asks reqwest for its default
;; features: a known C TLS source, waiting on its upstream) or `clean` (the
;; manifest was read and asks for no default and no native-tls feature). A
;; row is a reading of one published manifest, so it names an exact version;
;; a lock bump makes it stale and the new version unattributed, which is the
;; prompt to read the new manifest.
;;
;; WHEN SUI PUBLISHES A RELEASE WITH 66a289f: move the lock to it
;; (engenho-sui-typescape's `sui-eval = "0.1.0"` admits any 0.1.x), run
;; `gen build`, and delete the sui-store row. If the banned crates have left
;; Cargo.lock this check then fails on the `clean` rows; delete those too,
;; and from then on it is a plain ban.
;;
;; WHEN GIX-TRANSPORT STOPS ASKING FOR reqwest's `rustls`: whether that is a
;; sui-eval release built on a gix that asks for `rustls-tls`, or engenho
;; dropping sui-eval, `cargo tree -i aws-lc-rs` goes empty and aws-lc-rs and
;; aws-lc-sys leave Cargo.lock. Delete the three aws-lc tls:CARRIERS rows
;; then; `direct` no longer reaches them, so nothing else has to change.
;;
;; TIER: a CI gate, not a type. It reads the TOML cargo reads: a manifest
;; edited to ask for reqwest's defaults or rustls's, a new dependency that
;; pulls native-tls, OpenSSL or AWS-LC, or a lock bump to an unread dependent
;; or an unread carrier edge fails before merge. It does not see per-binary
;; feature resolution: Cargo.lock is a superset of every build, so it can
;; fail on a crate that one binary never links, and — the other direction —
;; it reports quinn-proto's aws-lc-rs edge, which no engenho build activates.
;; Carrier and attribution rows are hand-recorded readings of upstream
;; manifests and of `cargo tree -e features`, not something this check can
;; verify. It says nothing about C linked by other means (a `-sys` crate that
;; is not TLS, or a system library a build script finds).
;;
;; Exit codes: 0 clean · 1 a rule is broken · 3 nothing was examined (no
;; Cargo.lock package, or no workspace member manifest): the check proved
;; nothing, which is not the same as finding nothing wrong.
;;
;; Tests: ci/no-c-tls.test.tlisp (tlisp-test sets TLISP_TEST=1, which loads
;; these definitions and skips the main at the bottom). CI runs this check
;; only through that suite, in test.yml's ci-contract-tests: its first test
;; applies every rule to the real repository. Run the main locally:
;;   tatara-script ci/no-c-tls.tlisp

(define tls:MANIFEST "Cargo.toml")
(define tls:LOCK "Cargo.lock")

;; The native-tls family. native-tls is the reqwest backend; openssl and
;; openssl-sys are what it links on Linux; hyper-tls and tokio-native-tls are
;; reqwest's adapters to it.
(define tls:BANNED-NATIVE (list "openssl-sys" "openssl" "native-tls" "hyper-tls" "tokio-native-tls"))

;; The AWS-LC family. aws-lc-sys carries AWS-LC's C and compiles it with
;; `cc`; aws-lc-rs is its Rust binding, and rustls's default crypto provider.
;; The binding is banned beside the -sys crate for the same reason openssl is
;; banned beside openssl-sys: a new dependent then fails on its own edge,
;; not one level down where the name no longer says who asked.
(define tls:BANNED-AWS-LC (list "aws-lc-sys" "aws-lc-rs"))

(define tls:BANNED (append tls:BANNED-NATIVE tls:BANNED-AWS-LC))

;; What the ban is measured against: a rustls HTTP client in the resolve.
(define tls:REQUIRED (list "reqwest" "rustls" "hyper-rustls"))

;; Features of a guarded dependency that turn a C crypto stack on, as
;; (dependency feature why) rows. `default` is in each because naming it in a
;; feature list is the same as leaving that dependency's defaults on.
(define tls:C-FEATURES
  (list
    (list "reqwest" "default" "reqwest's defaults, `default-tls` among them")
    (list "reqwest" "default-tls" "native-tls in reqwest 0.12; in 0.13 it is `[\"rustls\"]`, which is aws-lc-rs")
    (list "reqwest" "native-tls" "native-tls, the backend that links OpenSSL")
    (list "reqwest" "native-tls-alpn" "native-tls, with ALPN")
    (list "reqwest" "native-tls-vendored" "native-tls, with its OpenSSL vendored")
    (list "reqwest" "rustls" "reqwest 0.13's `__rustls-aws-lc-rs` backend; the ring one is `rustls-tls`")
    (list "rustls" "default" "rustls's defaults, `aws_lc_rs` and `prefer-post-quantum` among them")
    (list "rustls" "aws-lc-rs" "aws-lc-rs, rustls's C crypto provider")
    (list "rustls" "aws_lc_rs" "aws-lc-rs, rustls's C crypto provider")
    (list "rustls" "prefer-post-quantum" "aws_lc_rs: rustls's X25519MLKEM768 preference is aws-lc-rs only")
    (list "rustls" "fips" "aws-lc-rs, in FIPS mode")))

;; What each guarded [workspace.dependencies] line must name, and why, as
;; (dependency (feature why) …). The dependency names here are the guarded
;; set: the member rules read the same list.
(define tls:WORKSPACE-LINES
  (list
    (list "reqwest"
      (list "rustls-tls" "the rustls backend, with the bundled webpki roots")
      (list "rustls-tls-native-roots" "the OS trust store native-tls read; without it a private CA stops verifying")
      (list "charset" "one of reqwest's defaults, named back so that TLS is the only thing the line turns off")
      (list "http2" "one of reqwest's defaults, named back so that TLS is the only thing the line turns off")
      (list "system-proxy" "one of reqwest's defaults, named back so that TLS is the only thing the line turns off"))
    (list "rustls"
      (list "ring" "the crypto provider engenho installs at boot, in place of rustls's default aws-lc-rs")
      (list "std" "one of rustls's defaults, named back so that the provider is the only thing the line turns off")
      (list "tls12" "one of rustls's defaults, named back so that the provider is the only thing the line turns off")
      (list "logging" "one of rustls's defaults, named back so that the provider is the only thing the line turns off"))))

;; Every read edge from a crate that is not itself C into one that is, as
;; (package version banned-crate why). See THE CARRIER TABLE above.
(define tls:CARRIERS
  (list
    (list "reqwest" "0.12.28" "native-tls"
      "feature `default-tls`; which dependent asks for it is in tls:ATTRIBUTION")
    (list "reqwest" "0.12.28" "hyper-tls"
      "feature `default-tls`; which dependent asks for it is in tls:ATTRIBUTION")
    (list "reqwest" "0.12.28" "tokio-native-tls"
      "feature `default-tls`; which dependent asks for it is in tls:ATTRIBUTION")
    (list "rustls" "0.23.40" "aws-lc-rs"
      "feature `aws_lc_rs` (one of rustls's defaults, and implied by `prefer-post-quantum`), turned on here by reqwest 0.13.4's `__rustls-aws-lc-rs` <- gix-transport 0.57.1 `http-client-reqwest-rust-tls` <- gix <- sui-eval 0.1.153")
    (list "rustls-webpki" "0.103.13" "aws-lc-rs"
      "feature `aws-lc-rs`, which rustls's `aws_lc_rs` enables as `webpki/aws-lc-rs`")
    (list "quinn-proto" "0.11.14" "aws-lc-rs"
      "its optional `aws-lc-rs` dependency, activated by no engenho build; Cargo.lock resolves every optional dependency, and `cargo tree -i aws-lc-rs` shows no quinn-proto branch")))

;; Every non-workspace reqwest dependent in Cargo.lock while a banned crate
;; is there, as (package version kind why). See THE ATTRIBUTION TABLE above.
(define tls:ATTRIBUTION
  (list
    (list "sui-store" "0.1.153" "asks"
      "[dependencies.reqwest] features = [rustls-tls, json, stream] with default features on; fixed in pleme-io/sui 66a289f, not in any release as of 0.1.219")
    (list "object_store" "0.13.2" "clean"
      "[dependencies.reqwest] default-features = false, features = [rustls-tls-native-roots, http2]")
    (list "tameshi" "0.1.3" "clean"
      "[dependencies.reqwest] default-features = false, features = [json, rustls-tls]")))

(define tls:KINDS (list "asks" "clean"))

;; The dependency tables cargo reads (the underscore spellings are its older,
;; still-accepted names).
(define tls:DEP-TABLES
  (list "dependencies" "dev-dependencies" "build-dependencies" "dev_dependencies" "build_dependencies"))

;; ── Reading parsed TOML ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
;; toml-parse yields a table as a list of (key value) and an array as a list.

(define (tls:get al key) (if (list? al) (alist-get al key "") ""))

(define (tls:has-key? al key)
  (and (list? al) (any? (fn (e) (and (list? e) (equal? (car e) key))) al)))

(define (tls:table al key) (let ((v (tls:get al key))) (if (list? v) v (list))))

(define (tls:as-list v) (if (list? v) v (list)))

(define (tls:in? x xs) (any? (fn (y) (equal? x y)) xs))

(define (tls:n x) (string-format "{}" x))

(define (tls:strings v) (filter (fn (s) (string? s)) (tls:as-list v)))

;; ── One guarded dependency entry ─────────────────────────────────────────

;; An entry's `default-features` as written: true, false, another value, or
;; "unset" when the key is absent.
(define (tls:default-features entry)
  (cond
    ((tls:has-key? entry "default-features") (tls:get entry "default-features"))
    ((tls:has-key? entry "default_features") (tls:get entry "default_features"))
    (else "unset")))

(define (tls:false? v) (and (boolean? v) (equal? v false)))
(define (tls:true? v) (and (boolean? v) (equal? v true)))

;; The dependencies these rules read: the ones tls:WORKSPACE-LINES governs.
(define tls:GUARDED (map car tls:WORKSPACE-LINES))

;; `dep`'s features that turn a C crypto stack on, and why one of them does.
(define (tls:c-features dep)
  (map (fn (r) (car (cdr r))) (filter (fn (r) (equal? (car r) dep)) tls:C-FEATURES)))

(define (tls:c-why dep f)
  (reduce (fn (acc r) (if (and (equal? (car r) dep) (equal? (car (cdr r)) f)) (car (cdr (cdr r))) acc))
    "" tls:C-FEATURES))

(define (tls:c-feature-line at dep f)
  (string-append at "names the feature `" f "`, which turns on " (tls:c-why dep f)))

;; Is the entry `key = value` a `dep` dependency? Either the key is `dep`
;; and no `package` renames it away, or `package = "<dep>"`.
(define (tls:dep-entry? dep key value)
  (let ((pkg (if (and (list? value) (tls:has-key? value "package")) (tls:get value "package") "")))
    (or (equal? pkg dep) (and (equal? key dep) (equal? pkg "")))))

;; Violations for one `dep` entry `key = entry`, found at `where`.
(define (tls:entry-violations where dep key entry)
  (let ((at (string-append where " `" key "`: ")))
    (cond
      ((string? entry)
        (list (string-append at "is the bare version \"" entry "\", which takes " dep "'s default features, a C crypto stack among them; write `{ workspace = true }` or a table with `default-features = false`")))
      ((not (list? entry))
        (list (string-append at "is neither a version nor a table")))
      (else
        (let* ((df (tls:default-features entry))
               (c (filter (fn (f) (tls:in? f (tls:c-features dep))) (tls:strings (tls:get entry "features")))))
          (append
            (cond
              ((tls:true? (tls:get entry "workspace"))
                (if (tls:true? df)
                  (list (string-append at "inherits the workspace line but sets `default-features = true`, which turns " dep "'s defaults, a C crypto stack among them, back on"))
                  (list)))
              ((tls:false? df) (list))
              (else
                (list (string-append at "does not set `default-features = false`, so it takes " dep "'s default features, a C crypto stack among them; inherit the workspace line with `workspace = true`"))))
            (map (fn (f) (tls:c-feature-line at dep f)) c)))))))

;; ── Rules over the manifests ─────────────────────────────────────────────

;; One [workspace.dependencies] line: `dep`, wanting each of `wants`.
(define (tls:line-violations manifest dep wants)
  (let* ((deps (tls:table (tls:table manifest "workspace") "dependencies"))
         (at (string-append tls:MANIFEST " [workspace.dependencies] " dep ": ")))
    (cond
      ((not (tls:has-key? deps dep))
        (list (string-append at "missing; the members' `workspace = true` entries would have no line to inherit")))
      ((not (list? (tls:get deps dep)))
        (list (string-append at "is a bare version, which takes " dep "'s default features, a C crypto stack among them")))
      (else
        (let* ((entry (tls:get deps dep))
               (features (tls:strings (tls:get entry "features"))))
          (append
            (if (tls:false? (tls:default-features entry))
              (list)
              (list (string-append at "needs `default-features = false` (the boolean); the `default` feature turns on " (tls:c-why dep "default"))))
            (map (fn (w) (string-append at "does not name `" (car w) "`, " (car (cdr w))))
              (filter (fn (w) (not (tls:in? (car w) features))) wants))
            (map (fn (f) (tls:c-feature-line at dep f))
              (filter (fn (f) (tls:in? f (tls:c-features dep))) features))))))))

(define (tls:workspace-violations manifest)
  (reduce (fn (acc line) (append acc (tls:line-violations manifest (car line) (cdr line))))
    (list) tls:WORKSPACE-LINES))

;; Every (where key entry) for a `dep` entry in one dependency table.
(define (tls:dep-entries dep where table)
  (map (fn (e) (list where (car e) (car (cdr e))))
    (filter (fn (e) (and (list? e) (string? (car e)) (tls:dep-entry? dep (car e) (car (cdr e))))) (tls:as-list table))))

;; Every (where key entry) for a `dep` entry anywhere in a member manifest.
(define (tls:manifest-entries dep path manifest)
  (append
    (reduce
      (fn (acc t) (append acc (tls:dep-entries dep (string-append path " [" t "]") (tls:table manifest t))))
      (list) tls:DEP-TABLES)
    (reduce
      (fn (acc target)
        (append acc
          (reduce
            (fn (acc2 t)
              (append acc2
                (tls:dep-entries dep (string-append path " [target." (tls:n (car target)) "." t "]")
                  (tls:table (car (cdr target)) t))))
            (list) tls:DEP-TABLES)))
      (list) (tls:table manifest "target"))))

;; A [features] value `<key>/<f>` or `<key>?/<f>` (for any key that names
;; `dep`) enabling one of `dep`'s C features.
(define (tls:feature-violations dep path manifest keys)
  (reduce
    (fn (acc feature)
      (append acc
        (map
          (fn (v)
            (string-append path " [features] `" (tls:n (car feature)) "` enables `" v
              "`, which turns on " (tls:c-why dep (car (cdr (string-split v "/"))))))
          (filter
            (fn (v)
              (let ((parts (string-split v "/")))
                (and (= (length parts) 2)
                     (tls:in? (string-replace (car parts) "?" "") keys)
                     (tls:in? (car (cdr parts)) (tls:c-features dep)))))
            (tls:strings (car (cdr feature)))))))
    (list)
    (filter (fn (f) (and (list? f) (string? (car f)))) (tls:table manifest "features"))))

;; Every rule over one member manifest, for one guarded dependency.
(define (tls:member-dep-violations dep path manifest)
  (let* ((entries (tls:manifest-entries dep path manifest))
         (keys (map (fn (e) (car (cdr e))) entries)))
    (append
      (reduce (fn (acc e) (append acc (tls:entry-violations (car e) dep (car (cdr e)) (car (cdr (cdr e)))))) (list) entries)
      (tls:feature-violations dep path manifest (if (tls:in? dep keys) keys (append keys (list dep)))))))

;; `members` is a list of (path parsed-manifest).
(define (tls:member-violations members)
  (reduce
    (fn (acc m)
      (append acc
        (reduce (fn (acc2 dep) (append acc2 (tls:member-dep-violations dep (car m) (car (cdr m)))))
          (list) tls:GUARDED)))
    (list) members))

;; ── Reading Cargo.lock ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
;; A package as (name version local? deps), each dep as (name version); a
;; dependency written without a version ("rustls") has version "", which
;; cargo writes only when one version of that package is locked.

(define (tls:dep-of s)
  (let ((parts (string-split s " ")))
    (list (car parts) (if (> (length parts) 1) (car (cdr parts)) ""))))

(define (tls:packages lock)
  (map
    (fn (p)
      (list (tls:get p "name") (tls:get p "version") (not (tls:has-key? p "source"))
        (map tls:dep-of (tls:strings (tls:get p "dependencies")))))
    (filter (fn (p) (list? p)) (tls:as-list (tls:get lock "package")))))

(define (tls:p-name p) (car p))
(define (tls:p-version p) (car (cdr p)))
(define (tls:p-local? p) (car (cdr (cdr p))))
(define (tls:p-deps p) (car (cdr (cdr (cdr p)))))
(define (tls:p-label p) (string-append (tls:p-name p) " " (tls:p-version p)))

(define (tls:banned-deps p) (filter (fn (d) (tls:in? (car d) tls:BANNED)) (tls:p-deps p)))

;; ── Rules over Cargo.lock ────────────────────────────────────────────────

(define (tls:present-violations pkgs)
  (map (fn (r) (string-append tls:LOCK ": no `" r "` package; the ban below would pass for want of a rustls HTTP client, not because of one"))
    (filter (fn (r) (not (any? (fn (p) (equal? (tls:p-name p) r)) pkgs))) tls:REQUIRED)))

;; Does a tls:CARRIERS row read this package's edge into `banned-name`?
(define (tls:carried? p banned-name carriers)
  (any?
    (fn (row)
      (and (equal? (car row) (tls:p-name p))
           (equal? (car (cdr row)) (tls:p-version p))
           (equal? (car (cdr (cdr row))) banned-name)))
    carriers))

(define (tls:direct-violations pkgs carriers)
  (reduce
    (fn (acc p)
      (append acc
        (map (fn (d) (string-append tls:LOCK ": " (tls:p-label p) " depends on `" (car d) "` directly, and no tls:CARRIERS row reads that edge; read the feature that opens it, or the crate pulls a C crypto stack in by itself"))
          (filter (fn (d) (not (tls:carried? p (car d) carriers))) (tls:banned-deps p)))))
    (list)
    (filter (fn (p) (not (tls:in? (tls:p-name p) tls:BANNED))) pkgs)))

;; The versions of reqwest that depend on a banned crate.
(define (tls:tainted-reqwests pkgs)
  (map tls:p-version
    (filter (fn (p) (and (equal? (tls:p-name p) "reqwest") (> (length (tls:banned-deps p)) 0))) pkgs)))

;; Does `p` depend on a reqwest in `tainted`? A versionless dependency names
;; the one reqwest in the lock, tainted exactly when `tainted` is non-empty.
(define (tls:uses-tainted? p tainted)
  (any?
    (fn (d)
      (and (equal? (car d) "reqwest")
           (if (equal? (car (cdr d)) "") (> (length tainted) 0) (tls:in? (car (cdr d)) tainted))))
    (tls:p-deps p)))

(define (tls:row-label row) (string-append (car row) " " (car (cdr row))))
(define (tls:row-kind row) (car (cdr (cdr row))))

(define (tls:row-matches? row p)
  (and (equal? (car row) (tls:p-name p)) (equal? (car (cdr row)) (tls:p-version p))))

;; Violations of `attributed` and `stale`, over the rows `rows`. The table
;; reads dependents of the reqwest that carries native-tls, so its liveness
;; keys on tls:BANNED-NATIVE: the aws-lc family arrives through rustls, on a
;; reqwest the workspace line does not pin, and is recorded in tls:CARRIERS.
(define (tls:attribution-violations pkgs rows)
  (let* ((banned (filter (fn (p) (tls:in? (tls:p-name p) tls:BANNED-NATIVE)) pkgs))
         (tainted (tls:tainted-reqwests pkgs))
         (dependents (filter (fn (p) (and (not (tls:p-local? p)) (tls:uses-tainted? p tainted))) pkgs))
         (live (filter (fn (row) (any? (fn (p) (tls:row-matches? row p)) dependents)) rows))
         (at (string-append tls:LOCK ": ")))
    (append
      (map (fn (row) (string-append "tls:ATTRIBUTION row " (tls:row-label row) ": kind `" (tls:n (tls:row-kind row)) "` is neither asks nor clean"))
        (filter (fn (row) (not (tls:in? (tls:row-kind row) tls:KINDS))) rows))
      (if (= (length banned) 0)
        (map (fn (row) (string-append "tls:ATTRIBUTION row " (tls:row-label row) " is stale: no C TLS crate is in " tls:LOCK "; delete it, or it would excuse the next regression"))
          rows)
        (append
          (map
            (fn (p)
              (string-append at (tls:p-label p) " depends on a reqwest that carries native-tls, and has no tls:ATTRIBUTION row; read its reqwest line: asking for the default features makes it a new C TLS source"))
            (filter (fn (p) (not (any? (fn (row) (tls:row-matches? row p)) rows))) dependents))
          (map
            (fn (row)
              (string-append "tls:ATTRIBUTION row " (tls:row-label row) " is stale: no package at that version depends on a reqwest that carries native-tls; delete it, or read the version that is locked now"))
            (filter (fn (row) (not (tls:in? row live))) rows))
          (if (any? (fn (row) (equal? (tls:row-kind row) "asks")) live)
            (list)
            (list (string-append at "C TLS is locked (" (string-join ", " (map tls:p-label banned))
                    ") and no attributed dependent asks reqwest for its defaults; a `clean` row is wrong, or the source is not a reqwest dependent"))))))))

(define (tls:lock-violations lock rows carriers)
  (let ((pkgs (tls:packages lock)))
    (append (tls:present-violations pkgs) (tls:direct-violations pkgs carriers) (tls:attribution-violations pkgs rows))))

;; ── Everything, as one list of violation lines ──────────────────────────

(define (tls:violations manifest members lock rows carriers)
  (append
    (tls:workspace-violations manifest)
    (tls:member-violations members)
    (tls:lock-violations lock rows carriers)))

;; ── Loading the real files ───────────────────────────────────────────────

(define (tls:load-toml path) (if (path-exists? path) (toml-parse (read-file path)) (list)))

(define (tls:load-manifest) (tls:load-toml tls:MANIFEST))

(define (tls:load-lock) (tls:load-toml tls:LOCK))

;; Each workspace member as (path parsed-manifest). A member whose manifest
;; cannot be read is reported by tls:unread-members, never skipped quietly.
(define (tls:member-paths manifest)
  (map (fn (m) (string-append m "/Cargo.toml")) (tls:strings (tls:get (tls:table manifest "workspace") "members"))))

(define (tls:load-members manifest)
  (map (fn (p) (list p (toml-parse (read-file p)))) (filter (fn (p) (path-exists? p)) (tls:member-paths manifest))))

(define (tls:unread-members manifest)
  (map (fn (p) (string-append p ": a workspace member with no manifest here; this check reads every member's"))
    (filter (fn (p) (not (path-exists? p))) (tls:member-paths manifest))))

(define (tls:main)
  (let* ((manifest (tls:load-manifest))
         (members (tls:load-members manifest))
         (lock (tls:load-lock))
         (pkgs (tls:packages lock))
         (vs (append (tls:unread-members manifest) (tls:violations manifest members lock tls:ATTRIBUTION tls:CARRIERS)))
         (banned (filter (fn (p) (tls:in? (tls:p-name p) tls:BANNED)) pkgs)))
    (when (or (= (length pkgs) 0) (= (length members) 0))
      (print-line (string-append "FATAL: no package in " tls:LOCK ", or no member manifest; the check proved nothing"))
      (exit 3))
    (for-each (fn (v) (print-line (string-append "FAIL " v))) vs)
    (print-line
      (string-append "no-c-tls: " (tls:n (length pkgs)) " locked packages · " (tls:n (length members)) " member manifests · "
        "C crypto locked: " (if (= (length banned) 0) "none" (string-join ", " (map tls:p-label banned)))
        " · carrier rows: " (tls:n (length tls:CARRIERS))
        " · attribution rows: " (tls:n (length tls:ATTRIBUTION)) " · violations: " (tls:n (length vs))))
    (exit (if (> (length vs) 0) 1 0))))

(cond
  ((equal? (env-get "TLISP_TEST" "") "1") (print-line "TLISP_TEST=1: no-c-tls loaded; main skipped"))
  (else (tls:main)))
